      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
        LIC Claim for a Minor Nominee: Appointee, Guardian &amp; How the Money Is Held Till 18 (2026)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        Short answer: <strong>yes, your minor child can be the nominee — but LIC will not pay the claim money to the child directly.</strong> A person under 18 cannot legally receive or manage insurance money, so the law gives you a tool for exactly this situation: under Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938, when the nominee is a minor, the policyholder must appoint an adult <strong>appointee</strong> who receives the money on the child's behalf and holds it in trust until the child turns 18. LIC's own policy guidelines say the same thing — "where the nominee is a minor, an appointee has to be appointed to receive the monies in the event of the assured's death during the minority of the nominee." The claim itself is not smaller or slower because the nominee is a child; what breaks minor-nominee claims is the missing appointee. If no appointee was named, the money waits while the family gets a guardian recognised — a natural guardian, or a guardian appointed by the court — and in the worst cases, when both parents die together, that wait stretches into months or years. Everything below was re-checked on 24 November 2026 against Section 39 of the Insurance Act, LIC's claim-form requirements, and IRDAI's claim-settlement timelines.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Quick Tool CTA -->
    <div class="bg-brand-50 border border-brand-200 rounded-2xl p-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-sm font-bold text-brand-900">First, work out what the claim should be worth</h2>
        <p class="text-xs text-brand-700">A minor-nominee claim pays the same death benefit as any other death claim — sum assured plus vested bonuses — so calculate the figure before the appointee files, and compare it with what the branch offers.</p>
      </div>
      <a
        href="/lic-death-benefit-calculator/"
        class="px-4 py-2 bg-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-700 text-white font-bold text-xs rounded-xl transition-colors shrink-0 shadow-sm"
      >
        Calculate Death Benefit &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none text-sm leading-relaxed space-y-6 text-slate-700">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">1. The Three Roles: Nominee, Appointee, Guardian</h2>
      <p>
        Families mix these three up constantly, and the confusion is what causes the delay. They are three different jobs, and one person can hold more than one of them — but only if the policy says so.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
        <table class="min-w-full text-xs border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Role</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Who it is</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">What they do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Nominee</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">The person named in the policy to receive the money — here, the minor child.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">The money is theirs. If the child is your spouse, parent or child, they are a <strong>beneficial nominee</strong> under Section 39(7) and own the proceeds — the appointee only collects on their behalf.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Appointee</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">An adult the policyholder names in the nomination, specifically because the nominee is a minor.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Receives the claim money from LIC while the nominee is under 18, holds it in trust, spends only on the child's welfare, and hands it over at 18.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Guardian</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">The child's natural guardian (father, then mother, under the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act, 1956) or a guardian appointed by the court under the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Steps in when no appointee was named. The natural guardian can usually claim on the child's behalf; if neither parent is available, the court must appoint someone before LIC can release the money.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The key point is about ownership. <strong>The appointee is a temporary custodian, never the owner of the funds.</strong> The appointee receives the money, manages it for the child's benefit, and must hand it over — with an account of how it was used — when the child turns 18. An appointee who treats the claim as their own money is misusing trust money, and the child (or their guardian) can hold them to account for it later. This is also why the appointee's role ends automatically at 18: once the nominee is an adult, they take full control of the funds themselves.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">2. Naming an Appointee — Do It While You Are Alive</h2>
      <p>
        The appointee is named at the same time as the nomination, and it costs nothing to get it right:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>At the time of buying the policy:</strong> the proposal form has a nomination section — fill in the minor nominee's name, date of birth and relationship, and in the same section give the appointee's name, age, address and relationship to the minor. LIC records both on the policy.</li>
        <li><strong>On an existing policy:</strong> file a nomination change (Form 3750) at the servicing branch and add the appointee's details in the same form. Our <a href="/guides/lic-change-nominee-form-3750/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">nominee-change guide</a> walks through Form 3750 step by step.</li>
        <li><strong>Who to pick:</strong> usually the other parent is the first choice — they are already responsible for the child's welfare. Grandparents, a responsible sibling, or a trusted relative are the usual alternatives. Pick someone financially responsible and younger than you if possible, so they are likely to still be around when the claim arises. Keep the appointee consistent with the guardian named in your Will, if you have one, so the two documents do not point at different people.</li>
        <li><strong>The appointee must be an adult</strong> (18 or older). You cannot appoint another minor as appointee. LIC's nomination form asks for the appointee's age precisely so it can check this.</li>
        <li><strong>Check it every few years.</strong> A nomination made when your child was five can still be sitting in the policy records when the child is 18 — and an appointee named a decade ago may have died, moved away, or fallen out with the family. Review the nomination after every marriage, birth, or death in the family, and get LIC's written acknowledgement of any change.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple minor nominees:</strong> you can name more than one nominee with a percentage split, and the same appointee can act for all of them — but say so clearly on the form, with the split in writing, so the branch does not have to guess.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">3. When the Claim Arises: What the Appointee Files</h2>
      <p>
        With an appointee named, the claim follows the ordinary death-claim route — the only difference is who signs and whose bank account receives the money. The appointee files the claim on the minor's behalf at the servicing branch (the branch that issued the policy), and LIC's forms page lists the standard death-claim requirements: Claim Form A (Form 3783, or 3783A if the policy has run three years or more), the discharge form (Form 3801), and the original policy document. For a minor nominee, add the documents in the table below.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
        <table class="min-w-full text-xs border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Document</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Death certificate of the life assured</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Issued by the municipal authority — the foundation document for every death claim.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Claim Form A — Form 3783 / 3783A</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Filled and signed by the appointee on the minor nominee's behalf; 3783A if the policy has run 3+ years.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Discharge Form 3801</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Executed by the appointee; the branch issues it. This is the appointee's receipt for the money.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Original policy bond</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">If lost, LIC's duplicate-bond procedure applies (see our <a href="/guides/lic-duplicate-policy-bond-form-3756/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">duplicate bond guide</a>).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Minor nominee's birth certificate</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Proves the nominee is a minor and the date on which they turn 18 — the branch works from this date.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Appointee's KYC + relationship proof</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">PAN, Aadhaar or other ID and address proof, plus proof of the appointee's relationship with the minor.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Appointee's NEFT mandate + cancelled cheque</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Bank account in the appointee's own name, for the claim to be credited — the money goes to the appointee, not to the child.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-800">Early-claim extras (if applicable)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">If death occurred within three years of the policy's start or revival, LIC also asks for the medical attendant's certificate (Form B), hospital certificate (Form B1) and identity/burial certificate (Form C); accidental death needs the FIR and post-mortem report.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Submit everything at the servicing branch and take a stamped acknowledgement listing each document. Under IRDAI's master circular on protection of policyholders' interests (5 September 2024), a life insurer must settle a death claim within 15 days of intimation where no investigation is needed, and within 45 days where one is — and if it misses the deadline it must pay interest at the bank rate plus 2% a year from the date of intimation, on its own initiative. The full document checklist for an ordinary death claim is in our <a href="/guides/lic-death-claim-documents-nominee/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">death claim documents guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">4. If No Appointee Was Named — The Guardian Route</h2>
      <p>
        This is the situation that stalls claims. LIC cannot pay a minor directly and there is nobody on the policy authorised to receive the money, so the family must first produce someone the law recognises as entitled to act for the child. Which route applies depends on who is still alive:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>The other parent is alive (the common case).</strong> The surviving parent is the child's natural guardian — under the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act, 1956, the father is the natural guardian, and after him the mother. The surviving parent files the claim on the child's behalf, with the child's birth certificate, their own ID and bank details, and proof of relationship. The branch may still ask questions — it will confirm the nomination and that no appointee exists — but this route does not normally need a court order. Get the branch's exact requirement in writing before assuming.</li>
        <li><strong>Neither parent is alive, or the surviving parent cannot act.</strong> Then a guardian must be appointed by the court under the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890. A family member applies to the District Court for a guardianship certificate, and LIC pays the court-appointed guardian on production of that certificate. This is real litigation — it takes months, needs a lawyer, and the court's main concern is the child's welfare, not the family's convenience. Budget for the time and the cost; there is no shortcut through the branch.</li>
        <li><strong>Both parents died together.</strong> The hardest case, and the one that makes the news: the spouses were often each other's nominees, so both nominations fail, and the surviving child is a minor with no appointee and no surviving parent. The claim then needs both a guardianship certificate and legal evidence of title (such as a succession certificate), because there is no valid nominee at all. This is exactly the scenario a named appointee prevents — one line on the nomination form, filled in years earlier, removes the entire court process.</li>
        <li><strong>The policy was assigned (for example, to a bank for a loan).</strong> Assignment under Section 38 overrides the nomination, so the claim first goes to the assignee up to the outstanding loan amount, and only the balance reaches the minor's appointee or guardian. See our <a href="/guides/lic-policy-assignment-bank-loan/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">policy assignment guide</a> for how that works.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">5. How the Money Is Held Till 18 — and What the Appointee May and May Not Do</h2>
      <p>
        LIC's job ends when it pays the appointee against valid documents. What happens to the money after that is governed by trust, not by LIC — and it is the part families should think about before naming anyone:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>The money is held in trust for the minor.</strong> The appointee is a fiduciary: they receive the money, hold it, and manage it solely for the child's benefit until the child turns 18. School fees, medical care, and the child's living costs are legitimate uses; the appointee's own debts, business, or lifestyle are not.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep it separate and keep records.</strong> The cleanest arrangement is a separate bank account in the appointee's name earmarked for the child, with every deposit and withdrawal recorded. When the child turns 18, the appointee should be able to show where every rupee went. Families that mix the claim into the appointee's household account are the ones that end up in disputes years later.</li>
        <li><strong>Invest it conservatively, in the child's name where possible.</strong> Money meant for a child who is years from adulthood should not sit idle in a savings account losing value to inflation, and it should not be gambled on risky investments either. Fixed deposits, or a minor's account with the appointee as guardian, are the usual choices — and any investment decision should be one the appointee can justify to the child at 18. This is a family decision, not insurance advice: talk to a financial adviser if the sum is large.</li>
        <li><strong>The appointee cannot sell, gift, or pledge the child's share.</strong> The claim money belongs to the minor nominee — and where the nominee is the policyholder's child, Section 39(7) makes them beneficially entitled to it, which means the money is theirs by law, not merely held for distribution among the legal heirs. Nobody else in the family can claim a share of it on the strength of being an heir.</li>
        <li><strong>If the appointee dies before the minor turns 18,</strong> the appointee's role ends with them — it does not pass to their heirs. The child's natural guardian takes over, or, if none is available, the court appoints a guardian (section 4). This is another reason to review the nomination regularly: a living, reachable appointee matters more than the name written ten years ago.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">6. What LIC Pays — the Same Death Benefit</h2>
      <p>
        The nominee being a minor changes who receives the money, not how much is paid. The claim is the ordinary death benefit: <strong>sum assured plus vested bonuses (and any final additional bonus)</strong>, exactly as if the nominee were an adult. Two points are worth knowing:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Accident benefit riders still apply</strong> where accidental death is proved — the FIR and post-mortem report are the evidence, and the extra benefit is paid to the appointee along with the main claim. (This is different from a missing-person claim, where no cause of death can be proved and no accident benefit follows.)</li>
        <li><strong>Tax on the claim:</strong> the death benefit is generally exempt under Section 10(10D) of the Income-tax Act, but the interest the appointee earns by investing the money is not the child's tax-free income — it is taxable in the hands of whoever earns it. Keep the tax paperwork with the claim records, and confirm the position with a tax adviser for large sums.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">7. Illustrative Example: ₹10 Lakh for a 10-Year-Old</h2>
      <p>
        The figures below are <strong>illustrative only</strong> — they show the maths, not a promise, and the branch's written quotation is final. Take a policy with a ₹10 lakh sum assured, where the holder dies and the nominee is their 10-year-old daughter, with her mother named as appointee:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>What LIC pays:</strong> ₹10 lakh sum assured, plus vested bonuses accrued over the policy's life — on a typical participating plan, a few lakh rupees more. The whole amount is credited to the mother's (appointee's) bank account by NEFT.</li>
        <li><strong>How it is held:</strong> the mother keeps it in a separate account for the daughter, uses it for school fees and living costs as needed, and records every withdrawal. The money is the daughter's — the mother is its custodian, not its owner.</li>
        <li><strong>At 18:</strong> the daughter takes full control of whatever remains, with the mother's records showing how it was used. If the mother had not been named appointee, the same money would have waited for a court-appointed guardian — and the family would have paid a lawyer and waited months before seeing a rupee.</li>
        <li><strong>If the same policy had no nominee at all:</strong> the claim would go to the legal heirs through a succession certificate — a 6-to-12-month process — instead of to the appointee in weeks.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">8. When the Minor Turns 18 — Handover and Clean-Up</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>The appointee's job ends on the 18th birthday.</strong> From that day the nominee is an adult and deals with LIC directly. Any money still held must be handed over, with the accounts.</li>
        <li><strong>Update the nomination on your own policies.</strong> If you named a minor child as nominee with an appointee years ago, the child's 18th birthday is the moment to file a fresh Form 3750: the appointee's role is over, and the now-adult child can be a direct nominee — and you can add a spouse or other dependants alongside them.</li>
        <li><strong>If the child was the nominee on a policy that has since matured,</strong> the maturity money is payable to the policyholder, not the nominee — nomination only matters on death during the term. Do not confuse the two.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the claim papers.</strong> The discharge form, the NEFT credit proof, and the appointee's records should be kept until the child is well past 18 — they are the proof of what was received and what was handed over, if anyone ever asks.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">9. If LIC Rejects the Claim or Delays It</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Ask for the rejection in writing, with reasons.</strong> A verbal "not payable" is not a decision. The branch must state the ground — no appointee on record, guardianship certificate required, documents short, policy lapsed — because each ground has a different answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Escalate inside LIC first:</strong> Branch Manager → Divisional Office → Zonal Office, then LIC's grievance portal. Keep copies of everything, with dates.</li>
        <li><strong>Bima Bharosa / IRDAI:</strong> IRDAI's Bima Bharosa portal takes complaints against insurers, and the Insurance Ombudsman can hear claim disputes. Our <a href="/guides/lic-complaint-ombudsman-bima-bharosa/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">complaint and ombudsman guide</a> walks through both routes, with timelines.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not pay a tout who promises a fast guardianship certificate.</strong> Court appointments take the time they take; anyone selling speed is selling a problem. Use a lawyer, or the District Legal Services Authority for free legal aid.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">10. Mistakes Families Make</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Naming a minor nominee and no appointee.</strong> The single most common mistake — and the one that turns a weeks-long claim into a court case. Check your policies today: if a child is the nominee, an appointee must be named in the same nomination.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming the surviving parent automatically gets the money.</strong> The surviving parent is the natural guardian and can usually claim on the child's behalf — but if the nomination is in someone else's name, or the policy was assigned, the money does not automatically go to the parent. Read the policy, don't assume.</li>
        <li><strong>Appointing someone without telling them.</strong> An appointee who first learns of the role at the branch, weeks after a death, will be slow, confused, and possibly unwilling. Tell the appointee now, and tell them where the policy papers are kept.</li>
        <li><strong>Never reviewing the nomination.</strong> Children grow up, appointees die or move away, marriages change the family. A nomination that was right ten years ago can be wrong today — review it after every major family event.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixing the child's money with household money.</strong> Once the claim lands in the appointee's account, keeping it separate is what protects both the child and the appointee from disputes later.</li>
        <li><strong>Forgetting the assignment.</strong> If the policy was assigned to a bank for a loan, the bank is paid first and the nominee gets only the balance — families that forget the assignment are always surprised by the smaller cheque.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 border-t border-slate-200 pt-4">
        This guide is independent educational content, not legal or insurance advice. Nomination and the appointee's role are governed by Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938 (as amended in 2015), natural guardianship by the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act, 1956, and court-appointed guardianship by the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890; LIC's position is its published claim-form requirements and policy guidelines, re-checked on 24 November 2026. Guardianship law and court procedure vary by personal law, state and court — confirm them with a lawyer before filing. The claim figures in section 7 are illustrative only; the servicing branch's written quotation is final.
      </p>
    </div>

